<!--
  Baseline enterprise segmentation policy: a deny-list of unreasonable zone-to-zone
  communications (NOT deny-by-default + whitelist). Anything not listed remains ALLOWED.
  Rules instantiate NIST guidance; no single rule is claimed as a verbatim NIST mandate.

  Grounding:
    SP 800-41r1  - deny-by-default, DMZ containment, visitor-network separation,
                   internal zone isolation, egress filtering
    SP 800-207   - no implicit trust by network location; limit lateral movement
    SP 800-92    - centralized log collection (server subnets NOT blocked from Security)
    SP 800-53r5  - SC-7 (boundary protection), SC-7(5) (deny-by-default at managed
                   interfaces), AC-4 (information-flow enforcement), AC-5 (separation
                   of duties), AC-6/AC-6(1) (least privilege / access to security functions)
    NISTIR 8228  - IoT cybersecurity risk management (risk-based isolation)

  SEGMENTATION ASSUMPTIONS (enforced by topology, NOT by the rules below)
    Some isolation is achieved BY CONSTRUCTION at the routing/segmentation layer and
    is therefore intentionally absent from this deny-list:
      * DMZ_Backend is a segment private to the WAF. Its subnet is reachable only
        over the WAF's connected interface and is NOT advertised into the internal
        IGP, so NO internal zone has a route to it. The backend is unreachable from
        the internal network by construction - the only path is Frontend -> Backend
        (the WAF reverse-proxy path). Consequently this file contains NO
        '<internal zone> is not authorized to access DMZ_Backend' rules: that
        property is provided by segmentation, the same way internal hosts are
        unreachable from the Internet because RFC1918 space is not routed outward.
        (hspl11 below still denies Internet->Backend as a defense-in-depth backstop,
        mirroring the redundant Internet->internal denies hspl1-10.)
      * DMZ_Backend has NO RETURN ROUTE into the internal network either (Option A:
        "the backend speaks only to the WAF"). Its only L3 neighbour is the WAF; it
        has a FORWARD path via edge_fw, but replies addressed to it are not routable
        (172.16.20.0/24 is absent from the internal IGP and edge_fw blackholes it).
        The backend's ONLY intended egress is one-way log shipping to the SIEM
        (UDP syslog is fire-and-forget - it needs no reply, so the missing return
        route does not impede it), which is why DMZ_Backend -> Security is left OPEN
        like every other server subnet (SP 800-92), and DMZ_Backend -> DMZ_Frontend
        (the reverse-proxy return leg) is left open. EVERY OTHER backend-sourced flow
        is explicitly dropped on the FORWARD leg at edge_fw: the sensitive/internal/
        Internet zones by hspl30-36 and the untrusted zones (Guests, Devices,
        Branch_Users) by hspl60-62, so a compromised backend cannot one-way pivot
        anywhere it has no business reaching. (Structural unreachability stops
        INBOUND and stops bidirectional sessions; the explicit egress denies are
        still required because the one-way FORWARD leg is not blocked by routing.)
    This mirrors SP 800-41r1 §3.1 (the DMZ back tier is a separate segment whose only
    neighbor is the proxy that fronts it) and SP 800-207 (micro-segmentation): prefer
    structural unreachability for high-value segments, with ACLs as a backstop only.

  Intentionally NOT denied (normal flows, left open):
    * Employees / Finance / Branch_Users -> Internet, Applications, Identity
    * Devices -> Internet          (IoT depends on cloud/other systems - NISTIR 8228)
    * trusted endpoint <-> peer endpoint
    * Identity -> Internet         (internal DNS forwarding to public resolver)
    * Security -> Internet         (SIEM threat-intel feeds)
    * server subnets -> Security   (log shipping to the SIEM - SP 800-92); this
                                    INCLUDES both DMZ tiers. The backend ships via
                                    one-way UDP syslog, which works despite its
                                    missing return route. Enforcement SHOULD pin
                                    these flows to the SIEM host and the syslog port
                                    (a DMZ-side collector that the SIEM pulls from is
                                    a higher-assurance alternative).
    * Frontend -> Backend          (WAF reverse-proxy path - the SOLE path to the
                                    backend, and reachable only because the WAF is
                                    directly connected to the backend segment)

  Name aliases: Backend = DMZ_Backend, Frontend = DMZ_Frontend (WAF),
  Devices = IoT_Devices, Security = Admin_Security, Management = Admin_Management.
-->
<hspl-list
	xmlns="http://itrust6g-project.eu/hspl"
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://itrust6g-project.eu/hspl hspl.xsd">

	<!-- Perimeter: the Internet may reach only the published frontend - SP 800-41r1 deny-by-default (§4) & §4.1.1; §3.1 DMZ model (Backend) / SP 800-53 SC-7 / SP 800-207 -->
	<hspl id="hspl1"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Security</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl2"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl3"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Employees</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl4"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Finance</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl5"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Guests</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl6"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Devices</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl7"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Applications</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl8"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Database</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl9"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Identity</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl10"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Branch_Users</object></hspl>
	<!-- Defense-in-depth backstop: Internet->Backend is already prevented by construction
	     (backend not routable from outside, no inbound DNAT to it); this deny is kept for
	     the same belt-and-suspenders reason as hspl1-10. -->
	<hspl id="hspl11"><subject>Internet</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>DMZ_Backend</object></hspl>

	<!-- Guests (untrusted) isolated from internal zones - SP 800-41r1 §3.3 (visitor-network separation) / SP 800-207 / SP 800-53 SC-7 -->
	<hspl id="hspl12"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Employees</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl13"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Finance</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl14"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Applications</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl15"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Database</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl16"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Identity</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl17"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Security</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl18"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl19"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Devices</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl20"><subject>Guests</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Branch_Users</object></hspl>

	<!-- Devices/IoT (untrusted) isolated from internal zones - NISTIR 8228 (prevent an IoT device being used to attack other hosts) / SP 800-41r1 §3.3 / SP 800-207 -->
	<hspl id="hspl21"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Employees</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl22"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Finance</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl23"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Applications</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl24"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Database</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl25"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Identity</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl26"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Security</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl27"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl28"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Guests</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl29"><subject>Devices</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Branch_Users</object></hspl>

	<!-- DMZ Backend EGRESS containment (anti-pivot/anti-beacon). NOTE: inbound isolation
	     of the backend is structural (see SEGMENTATION ASSUMPTIONS above); these rules
	     constrain the backend when it is the SOURCE, which segmentation does not cover -
	     the backend can still initiate outward via the WAF, so its egress is filtered at
	     edge_fw. SP 800-41r1 §2.1.1/§4.1.1 (egress) / SP 800-53 SC-7 & AC-4.
	     Untrusted-zone egress (Guests/Devices/Branch) is completed by hspl60-62. -->
	<hspl id="hspl30"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Employees</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl31"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Finance</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl32"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Applications</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl33"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Database</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl34"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Identity</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl35"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl36"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Internet</object></hspl>

	<!-- DMZ Frontend/WAF containment (defense-in-depth; only intended internal flow is Frontend->Backend, which is also the sole structural path to the backend) - SP 800-41r1 §3.1 & §2.1.9 / SP 800-53 SC-7 & AC-4. Untrusted-zone + Internet egress is completed by hspl63-66. -->
	<hspl id="hspl37"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Employees</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl38"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Finance</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl39"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Applications</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl40"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Database</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl41"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Identity</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl42"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>

	<!-- Crown jewels: outsider->sensitive zone (SP 800-41r1 §3.3 accounting-DB example, §2 sensitive functions); no direct DB access incl. Finance = least privilege / app-tier mediation (SP 800-53 AC-6, AC-4); Finance separation (SP 800-53 AC-5) -->
	<hspl id="hspl43"><subject>Employees</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Database</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl44"><subject>Finance</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Database</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl45"><subject>Branch_Users</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Database</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl46"><subject>Employees</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Finance</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl47"><subject>Branch_Users</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Finance</object></hspl>

	<!-- Security tooling reachable only by the admin plane - SP 800-53 AC-6/AC-6(1) (access to security functions), SC-7, AC-4 / SP 800-92 (server->SIEM preserved) / SP 800-41r1 §5.2.1 (analogy) -->
	<hspl id="hspl48"><subject>Employees</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Security</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl49"><subject>Finance</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Security</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl50"><subject>Branch_Users</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Security</object></hspl>

	<!-- Management console: nothing initiates into it - SP 800-53 SC-7 & AC-6 / SP 800-41r1 §5.1 (analogy) / SP 800-207 (control-plane separation) -->
	<hspl id="hspl51"><subject>Employees</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl52"><subject>Finance</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl53"><subject>Branch_Users</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl54"><subject>Applications</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl55"><subject>Database</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl56"><subject>Identity</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Management</object></hspl>

	<!-- Egress: servers must not initiate to the Internet (anti-beacon/anti-C2; topology/use-case derived) - SP 800-41r1 §2.1.1 & §4.1.1 / SP 800-53 SC-7 / SP 800-207 -->
	<hspl id="hspl57"><subject>Applications</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Internet</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl58"><subject>Database</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Internet</object></hspl>

	<!-- Admin plane must not originate to the Internet (privileged-workstation / PAW
	     hardening; anti-beacon for the control plane). Closes the intent-vs-enforcement
	     gap: the deny is already ENFORCED by admin_fw's default-deny egress (permit
	     mgmt->RFC1918, drop the rest), but until now had no HSPL intent to trace to.
	     Patching still flows via an internal update server (admin_fw permits
	     mgmt->RFC1918). SP 800-53 SC-7(5) (deny-by-default egress), SC-7, AC-6 (least
	     privilege) / SP 800-41r1 §2.1.1 & §4.1.1 (egress filtering) / SP 800-207
	     (control-plane separation) / NSA "Defend Privileges and Accounts",
	     Microsoft/NCSC privileged-access-workstation guidance (no Internet/email from
	     privileged hosts). -->
	<hspl id="hspl59"><subject>Management</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Internet</object></hspl>

	<!-- DMZ source-containment COMPLETION. A DMZ host (Internet-exposed) must not
	     initiate into ANY zone except its required flows: Frontend -> Backend (proxy),
	     Frontend/Backend -> Security (one-way log shipping), Backend -> Frontend
	     (return). The original hspl30-42 denied the DMZ tiers toward the sensitive/
	     internal zones but left the UNTRUSTED zones (Guests, Devices, Branch_Users)
	     and the WAF's Internet egress open - a lateral-movement / anti-beacon hole.
	     These rules close it so the stated DMZ containment is actually complete.
	     SP 800-41r1 §3.1 (DMZ hosts do not initiate into the trusted side) & §2.1.1/
	     §4.1.1 (egress filtering) / SP 800-53 SC-7, AC-4 / SP 800-207 (limit lateral
	     movement). Enforced on the FORWARD leg at edge_fw. -->
	<hspl id="hspl60"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Guests</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl61"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Devices</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl62"><subject>DMZ_Backend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Branch_Users</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl63"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Guests</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl64"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Devices</object></hspl>
	<hspl id="hspl65"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Branch_Users</object></hspl>
	<!-- WAF must not originate to the Internet: it serves inbound clients on
	     ESTABLISHED connections and proxies to the internal backend; it never needs
	     NEW outbound to the Internet, so blanket egress here would only be a C2/exfil
	     channel if the WAF is compromised. (If OCSP/CRL egress is ever needed,
	     whitelist the specific responder rather than reopening this.) Mirrors the
	     server anti-beacon denies hspl36/hspl57/hspl58. -->
	<hspl id="hspl66"><subject>DMZ_Frontend</subject><action>is not authorized to access</action><object>Internet</object></hspl>

</hspl-list>
